## 2. Target Market and Operating Assumptions

### Initial geography

Launch in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, with an architecture that can later expand to other Ethiopian cities.

### Currency

Use Ethiopian birr (ETB) as the primary currency. Design currency fields so additional currencies can be supported later.

### Customer behavior

Assume that many buyers prefer interacting with a salesperson over browsing a website and checking out independently.

Therefore:

- Buyers can order without creating a platform account.
- Salespeople can create orders on behalf of buyers, with their consent.
- SMS is the primary order and payment notification channel.
- Mobile-friendly web pages are used for receipt uploads, order confirmation, and optional tracking.
- The supplier remains responsible for product availability and delivery.
- Payment instructions clearly identify the verified supplier receiving the money.
- The platform must not require every customer to install an application.

### Internet and device constraints

Design for inconsistent connectivity and mobile-first use.

- Optimize dashboards for low-bandwidth connections.
- Support responsive mobile browsers.
- Preserve order form inputs when a connection temporarily fails.
- Prevent accidental duplicate order creation after retries.
- Make essential workflows usable on lower-end Android devices.
- Use lightweight pages for customers accessing links through SMS.
- Do not require a native mobile application for the MVP.

### Business assumptions to validate

Do not treat any of these as proven:

- Suppliers will accept commission agreements.
- Salespeople can consistently generate profitable sales.
- Customers will pay through the proposed payment workflow.
- SMS delivery will be reliable across all providers.
- A particular Ethiopian payment provider supports marketplace split settlement.
- Suppliers will pay commission reserves or platform subscriptions.
- Customers will accept the proposed delivery and return conditions.

Build a testable MVP that helps validate these assumptions before investing in complex integrations.

